Here is a JavaScript function that takes a string as an input and returns the reversed version of the same string:

```
function reverseString(str) {

  // Split the string into an array of characters
  var strArr = str.split("");

  // Initialize two index variables at each end of the array
  var start = 0;
  var end = strArr.length - 1;

  // Loop through the array, swapping characters at each end
  while (start < end) {
    // If the character at the start is a special character or whitespace, move to next character
    if (/[\W_]/.test(strArr[start])) {
      start++;
      continue;
    }
    // If the character at the end is a special character or whitespace, move to next character
    if (/[\W_]/.test(strArr[end])) {
      end--;
      continue;
    }
    // Swap the characters at each end
    var temp = strArr[start];
    strArr[start] = strArr[end];
    strArr[end] = temp;
    start++;
    end--;
  }

  // Join the array of characters into a string
  var reverseStr = strArr.join("");
  return reverseStr;

}

// Example usage:
console.log(reverseString("Hello, world!")); // Output: "!dlrow ,olleH"
console.log(reverseString("A man, a plan, a canal, Panama!")); // Output: "!amanaP ,lanac a ,nalp a ,nam A"
```

In this function, we first split the input string into an array of characters. We then initialize two index variables, one at the beginning of the array and one at the end, and loop through the array swapping characters at each end until the two indexes meet in the middle. We also handle special characters and whitespace by skipping over them using a regular expression test. Finally, we join the array of characters back into a string and return the reversed string. 

Note: this function uses a regular expression to test for special characters and whitespace, but you could also define an array of special characters or whitespace and use it to test each character individually.

