```c++
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int binarySearch(int arr[], int n, int x) {
    int left = 0;
    int right = n - 1;
    while (left <= right) {
        int mid = (left + right) / 2;
        if (arr[mid] == x) {
            return mid;
        } else if (arr[mid] < x) {
            left = mid + 1;
        } else {
            right = mid - 1;
        }
    }
    return -1;
}

int main() {
    int arr[] = {1, 3, 5, 7, 9};
    int n = sizeof(arr)/sizeof(arr[0]);
    int x = 7;
    int result = binarySearch(arr, n, x);
    if (result == -1) {
        cout << "Element not found" << endl;
    } else {
        cout << "Element found at index " << result << endl;
    }
    return 0;
}
```

In this program, the `binarySearch` function takes in an array `arr`, the size of the array `n`, and the element we're searching for `x`. The function then performs a binary search on the array by setting `left` and `right` indices to the first and last positions of the array respectively. It continues to search for the element by calculating the `mid` index using `(left + right) / 2` and comparing the element at `mid` with `x`. If the two are equal, it returns `mid` as the index where the element was found. If the element at `mid` is less than `x`, the search focuses on the right half of the array by setting `left` to `mid + 1`. Otherwise, the search focuses on the left half of the array by setting `right` to `mid - 1`. This process continues until the element is found or the entire array has been searched. If the element is not found, the function returns `-1`.

In the `main` function, an example array `arr` and element `x` are created, and the `binarySearch` function is called with these parameters. The return value is then checked to see if the element was found or not, and the appropriate message is printed.

