The program handles any `IOException` that may occur while opening or reading the file, as well as any `NumberFormatException` that may occur while parsing the numeric fields. If such an error occurs, the program prints an error message and exits with a non-zero status code (2).

Here's an example function that performs a depth-first search on a graph represented by an adjacency list:

```
function depthFirstSearch(graph, startNode) {
  // Keep track of visited nodes
  const visited = {};
  // Initialize output array
  const result = [];

  // Define the recursive DFS function
  function dfs(node) {
    // Mark node as visited
    visited[node] = true;
    // Add node to output array
    result.push(node);
    // Visit all the neighbors of this node
    for (const neighbor of graph[node]) {
      // If neighbor hasn't been visited, visit it
      if (!visited[neighbor]) {
        dfs(neighbor);
      }
    }
  }

  // Perform the initial DFS with the starting node
  dfs(startNode);

  // Return the output array
  return result;
}
```

This function takes in two arguments: the graph (as an adjacency list) and the node to start the search from. It uses recursion to implement the DFS algorithm, marking each node as visited and adding it to an output array as it goes.

Note that this function assumes that the graph is connected, so if there are multiple disconnected components in the graph, it will only visit nodes in one of them. To search the entire graph, you could call this function on each unvisited node, like so:

```
function depthFirstSearchFull(graph) {
  // Keep track of visited nodes
  const visited = {};
  // Initialize output array
  const result = [];

  // Perform DFS on each unvisited node
  for (const node in graph) {
    if (!visited[node]) {
      dfs(node);
    }
  }

  // Define the recursive DFS function
  function dfs(node) {
    // Mark node as visited
    visited[node] = true;
    // Add node to output array
    result.push(node);
    // Visit all the neighbors of this node
    for (const neighbor of graph[node]) {
      // If neighbor hasn't been visited, visit it
      if (!visited[neighbor]) {
        dfs(neighbor);
      }
    }
  }

  // Return the output array
  return result;
}
```

